---
import fs from 'node:fs';
import path from 'node:path';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// The photo deck renders only when real images exist in public/photos/.
// Until then the section is simply omitted, so the build never ships broken
// <img> tags. Drop family-1/2/3.jpg (or .webp) in there and it appears.
const photoDir = path.join(process.cwd(), 'public', 'photos');
const photos = fs.existsSync(photoDir)
  ? fs
      .readdirSync(photoDir)
      .filter((f) => /^family-\d+\.(jpe?g|png|webp)$/i.test(f))
      .sort()
  : [];
const photoAlt = [
  'Javier with his family',
  'Rambi and Coco',
  'Javier with his dogs in Los Angeles',
];
---

<Base
  title="About · Javier Saldana"
  description="Platform engineer working on identity and authorization infrastructure. How I got here, how I work, and what I do when I'm not working."
>
  <p class="label">About</p>
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p class="meta resume-line">
    <a href="/Javier_Saldana_Resume.pdf">Resume (PDF)</a> ·
    <a href="https://github.com/saldanaj27">GitHub</a> ·
    <a href="https://linkedin.com/in/javier-saldana27">LinkedIn</a> ·
    <a href="mailto:saldanaj27@gmail.com">saldanaj27@gmail.com</a>
  </p>

  <p>
    I'm a platform engineer working on identity and authorization
    infrastructure. Every professional system I have shipped is a shared
    primitive consumed by other engineering teams: authentication services,
    authorization policy distribution, Kubernetes operators with CRDs as
    declarative APIs, a UID generation service. My users are other engineers,
    which is the part of the job I like most.
  </p>

  <p>
    I'm originally from Los Angeles and moved to New York for college in 2018.
    I went in for math, because calculus was the thing I was good at and
    reliably enjoyed, and then I found computer science and recognized it: the
    same logic puzzles I had been doing since I was a kid, except the board was
    infinite. That is still the pull. There is an ocean of things to learn in
    this field, and I have never once run out of the next thing.
  </p>

  <p>
    I joined FICO's platform organization as an intern in 2021, returned full
    time in 2022, and was promoted to Lead Software Engineer in 2026, all on the
    same identity and access management platform. Joining a brand-new platform
    initiative early meant I watched, and then increasingly shaped, systems
    growing from first gRPC APIs into infrastructure serving enterprise
    financial institutions. Lately the most interesting work has been where that
    infrastructure meets AI: multi-agent workflows running in production CI/CD,
    where the hard problems are the platform ones (identity, permissions,
    orchestration, failure recovery) rather than the model ones.
  </p>

  <h2>How I work</h2>
  <p>
    Some patterns recur often enough across my work that I trust them as
    descriptions rather than aspirations.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>I gravitate to zero-to-one infrastructure.</strong> Two Kubernetes
      operators, a UID service, a feature-flag module, and two complete side
      projects, all built from scratch. I prefer owning a service end to end
      (design, implementation, deployment, observability, on-call) over
      contributing features to someone else's.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>I think in trade-offs, not solutions.</strong> Every design
      decision names what it bought and what it cost. Stateless versus stateful,
      eventual versus strong consistency, availability versus correctness: the
      interesting engineering lives in what you knowingly gave up, which is why
      every project page on this site has a trade-offs section.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Stateless by default.</strong> The UID service has no database,
      the authentication facade has no database, and authorization decisions
      run against locally cached policy. I add state only when a design forces
      it, because state is where availability and consistency problems live.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>I automate incident categories, not incidents.</strong> The best
      outcome I know is making a class of failure structurally impossible: the
      RBAC operator ended provisioning misconfigurations by making correct
      configuration the only expressible kind.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>I write things down.</strong> Design decisions, trade-off records,
      honest reflections on what I'd do differently. Infrastructure work is
      judged over years, and undocumented reasoning doesn't survive that long.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Outside work</h2>

  <p>
    I'm Peruvian-American and the second in my family to go to college, after an
    older sister who is now in residency. Home is my parents, my sister, and two
    dogs I adore: Rambi and Coco, both past sixteen, both in Los Angeles, both
    entirely aware that their needs outrank mine.
  </p>

  {
    photos.length > 0 && (
      <figure class="family">
        <div class="deck" role="group" aria-label="Family photos">
          {photos.map((file, idx) => (
            <img
              src={`/photos/${file}`}
              alt={photoAlt[idx] ?? 'Family photo'}
              width="640"
              height="480"
              loading="lazy"
              decoding="async"
            />
          ))}
        </div>
        <figcaption>The people and dogs in question.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    )
  }

  <p>
    I'm a college football fan, specifically USC. I did not go to USC. I grew up
    in LA cheering for them, which is the whole explanation and, I'd argue,
    enough of one.
  </p>

  <p>
    Those who know me best know I spend a lot of my time playing Minesweeper. It
    is the same appeal that pulled me into computer science: a constrained
    board, incomplete information, and a solution that yields to reasoning
    instead of speed (though for the record, my time to beat on a 16x30 board is
    69.12 seconds). I also play soccer, and I'm working on running more
    consistently.
  </p>

  <p>
    Fun fact: I was once a licensed scuba diver (stopped after realizing I am
    afraid of the deep ocean).
  </p>

  <h2>What I'm looking for</h2>
  <p>
    Platform services, distributed systems, and infrastructure that other
    engineers build on: internal developer platforms, service meshes, policy
    engines, orchestration, AI infrastructure. I'm most useful on teams whose
    customers are other engineering teams, and I'm deliberately open to domains
    beyond identity. If that sounds like your team,
    <a href="mailto:saldanaj27@gmail.com">say hello</a>.
  </p>

  <style>
    .resume-line {
      margin-top: 0.5rem;
      margin-bottom: 2rem;
    }

    /* Photo deck: overlapping cards that cycle focus on a timer, like a hand
       of cards being fanned. Hover or focus lifts one to the front. Degrades
       to a plain row when JS is off, and holds still under reduced motion. */
    .family {
      max-width: var(--measure);
    }
    .deck {
      display: flex;
      align-items: flex-end;
      padding: 0.5rem 0 1rem;
      min-height: 0;
    }
    .deck img {
      width: clamp(9rem, 30%, 13rem);
      aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
      object-fit: cover;
      border: 1px solid var(--rule);
      border-radius: 4px;
      background: var(--bg-subtle);
      box-shadow: 0 2px 10px rgb(0 0 0 / 12%);
      transition:
        transform 420ms cubic-bezier(0.22, 0.61, 0.36, 1),
        box-shadow 420ms ease,
        filter 420ms ease;
      transform-origin: bottom center;
      filter: saturate(0.92);
      will-change: transform;
    }
    .deck img + img {
      margin-left: -2.75rem;
    }
    .deck img:nth-child(1) {
      rotate: -4deg;
    }
    .deck img:nth-child(2) {
      rotate: 1.5deg;
    }
    .deck img:nth-child(3) {
      rotate: 5deg;
    }
    /* The card currently in focus, set on a timer by the script below. */
    .deck img.is-front {
      z-index: 3;
      transform: translateY(-0.5rem) scale(1.06);
      box-shadow: 0 8px 24px rgb(0 0 0 / 20%);
      filter: saturate(1);
    }
    /* Hover and keyboard focus always win over the timer. */
    .deck img:hover,
    .deck img:focus-visible {
      z-index: 4;
      transform: translateY(-0.75rem) scale(1.09);
      box-shadow: 0 10px 28px rgb(0 0 0 / 24%);
      filter: saturate(1);
    }
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
      .deck img,
      .deck img.is-front,
      .deck img:hover,
      .deck img:focus-visible {
        transform: none;
        transition: none;
      }
    }
    @media (max-width: 30rem) {
      .deck img + img {
        margin-left: -1.75rem;
      }
    }
  </style>

  <script is:inline>
    (() => {
      const deck = document.querySelector('.deck');
      if (!deck) return;
      const cards = Array.from(deck.querySelectorAll('img'));
      if (cards.length < 2) return;

      const reduced = matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
      let i = 0;
      let timer = null;

      const show = (n) => {
        cards.forEach((c, idx) => c.classList.toggle('is-front', idx === n));
      };

      const start = () => {
        if (timer || reduced.matches) return;
        timer = setInterval(() => {
          i = (i + 1) % cards.length;
          show(i);
        }, 2600);
      };
      const stop = () => {
        clearInterval(timer);
        timer = null;
      };

      show(0);
      if (!reduced.matches) start();

      // Pause while a visitor is interacting, and when the tab is hidden.
      deck.addEventListener('pointerenter', stop);
      deck.addEventListener('pointerleave', start);
      deck.addEventListener('focusin', stop);
      deck.addEventListener('focusout', start);
      document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () =>
        document.hidden ? stop() : start()
      );
      reduced.addEventListener('change', () => (reduced.matches ? stop() : start()));
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
